# cinder_viz.conf — OPTIONAL. The ONLY thing this file does is TURN THE VISUALISER OFF.
#
# You do not need it to enable anything. Since 2026-07-27 the visualiser is ON by default and is
# driven entirely by Sony's built-in spectrum analyzer (AudioAnalyzerService) — there is no longer
# a synthetic fallback, because one animated identically for silence, a ballad and a drum solo, so
# it looked like a representation of the audio without being one. With no analyzer there are simply
# no bars.
#
# It is also no longer started at boot. cinder-home starts the analyzer ON DEMAND and only while
# ALL of these hold: the panel is on, Now Playing is the current screen, and audio is actually
# playing. It stops the moment any of them stops being true. So a Sony-service connect is off the
# boot path entirely (by the time it can run, the app has painted and cleared the bad-boot counter),
# and there is no FFT, no IPC and no wakeups while the screen is dark or you are browsing.
#
# ── TO DISABLE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Copy this file to /contents/cinder_viz.conf. The check is a deliberately dumb substring match for
# the exact text `analyzer=0`, so a malformed file can never do anything except disable the feature.

analyzer=0

# ── TO RE-ENABLE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DELETE /contents/cinder_viz.conf, or change the line above to `analyzer=1`.
#
# Deleting is the reliable move. Do NOT assume that some other value turns it back on: the match
# looks for `analyzer=0` anywhere in the file, so a stray commented-out `# analyzer=0` further down
# would keep it disabled. (An older version of this file said "change the line to anything other
# than analyzer=1" — that instruction was wrong and would have left the visualiser off.)
#
# The setting is read once and cached; it is re-read after a USB mass-storage session, which is the
# only way the file can change without a reboot.
#
# ── VALIDATING THE ANALYZER ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# If the visualiser shows nothing, the analyzer is the thing to check. This has NEVER been run on
# hardware — see docs/PRODUCTION_READINESS.md:
#
#     adb push cinder-home/dist/dev/cinder-probe /data/local/tmp/
#     # start music playing on the device, then:
#     adb shell 'cd /data/local/tmp && \
#       LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/system/vendor/sony/lib:/system/vendor/unknown321/lib:/system/lib:/usr/lib:/lib \
#       ./cinder-probe --analyzer'
#
# A PASS prints "frames flowed" plus the raw band values. If you see "started but NO frames", try
# `./cinder-probe --analyzer 0` (LEVEL mode) and confirm audio is really playing. Note the raw band
# range it prints — spectrum::from_bands in cinder-ffi auto-detects dBFS (any negative value) vs
# linear magnitude, so most encodings work as-is, but a range far outside 0..(a few hundred) linear
# or 0..-60 dB is worth calibrating against.
